>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The best mathematics career options after a BSc or MSc in maths are the ones where your marksheet already meets the entry rule and you can show proof of skill before the exam years or fees pile up.</strong>
    A maths degree does not pick the job. It decides which doors open. Some accept any maths graduate, such as the actuarial entrance and data work. Others quietly ask for more: the Indian Statistical Service wants statistics, college teaching wants a master's plus NET, and DRDO's cryptology lab wants a first-division master's. Check the gate first. Then build a <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> on top of the degree, because those are the decisions that unlock high income opportunities and bring earlier financial freedom closer.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Maths graduates have eight real routes. Lead with the ones where your skill sells directly: data and analytics, tutoring and content, quant finance, cryptography and optimisation. Keep the exam ladders (actuarial, research, government statistics) but pair each with a second skill.</li>
      <li>Read the gate before the glamour. A pure maths degree can fail the Indian Statistical Service and RBI's statistics stream, and a BSc alone does not reach college teaching or the DRDO cryptology fellowship.</li>
      <li>Add an MSc only when it names a door, and keep college spending near 10 percent of your education budget unless you have course-specific proof of more. The decisions that unlock high income opportunities are skill-stacking ones: statistics, code, a domain and visible proof.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> finish one test piece, then show it to three people who work in the route you are leaning towards.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
