  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      <strong>Backup rule for every exam route:</strong> keep one skill running that is useful if the exam does not work out, such as Python with SQL, or a small tutoring practice. An attempt history is not proof of work. A finished analysis is.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bsc-or-msc">BSc only, or MSc too: name the door first</h2>

  <p>
    The question is not whether an MSc is good. It is which named door it opens and what that door pays you for the cost.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Stop at the BSc and build skills if</div>
      <ul>
        <li>You are aiming at data, analytics, tutoring, optimisation or actuarial papers.</li>
        <li>You have money pressure at home and a skill-led job can start soon.</li>
        <li>You can show one or two finished projects and explain them clearly.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Add the MSc when it names a door</div>
      <ul>
        <li>College teaching with NET, the DRDO cryptology fellowship or the RBI statistics stream.</li>
        <li>Research, where ISI, an IISER or a similar institute is the route to a funded PhD.</li>
        <li>A role that lists the master's as a hard requirement in the advertisement.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  
    <ol class="rich-list">
      <li><strong>Count the full cost.</strong> A rule to start with: spend about 10 percent of your family's total education budget on college. If the budget is Rs 10 lakh, that is about Rs 1 lakh, and the other Rs 9 lakh stays for living costs, a laptop and internet, books, projects and later upskilling.</li>
      <li><strong>Treat anything above roughly 20 percent as a warning.</strong> It needs course-specific proof: a required credential, current teaching, verified outcomes and a realistic income after the full cost.</li>
      <li><strong>Prefer public seats reached by national tests.</strong> Avoid education debt unless the programme names a door you cannot reach another way. Both numbers are planning rules, not laws.</li>
    </ol>

  <p>
    College is only a small part of career success here. What gets you paid in most of these routes is Python, statistics, clear writing and a visible project. Free lectures on YouTube, NPTEL and university open courseware cover much of the content, so judge a paid course by what it adds: feedback, structure and a finished piece of work, not the certificate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pure-first">Pure maths first: ISI, CMI and the research ladder</h2>

  <p>
    If the part of maths you love is proof and structure, a research-first institute can be worth the effort. The Indian Statistical Institute lists B.Stat, B.Math and BSDS at undergraduate level, and Master of Statistics, Master of Mathematics and other master's programmes, through its admission test, which was held on 10 May 2026 in the last cycle. Chennai Mathematical Institute admits through its own entrance exam. Both publish eligibility and dates on their own sites, and sources disagree on small details, so read the current page.
  </p>

  <p>
    The National Board for Higher Mathematics runs a written test and interview for scholarships, and several institutes also use its score to screen applicants: IISER Bhopal, Mohali, Pune, Berhampur and Thiruvananthapuram, NISER, IMSc, HRI, Kerala School of Mathematics and Krea University. Papers from 2005 onward are public with keys, which makes them a free first test.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A strong peer group and a good supervisor can matter a lot in research. Prestige alone does not pay a loan. Choose the institute for the course and the people, and keep the funding questions answered in writing: stipend, duration, renewal and what happens if the project changes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai">What AI changes in routine maths and data work</h2>

  <p>
    A TeamLease analysis of more than 1,000 employers, reported in December 2025, expects firms to hire fewer people in 2026 and pay more for those who work well with AI. It also says employers want freshers who know when not to trust AI and can question its output. That is a maths skill.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Task</th>
          <th>What AI does now</th>
          <th>Where your maths still wins</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
