            <td data-label="Where your maths still wins">{item.edge}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The World Economic Forum's Future of Jobs Report 2025, as reported in the press, lists big data specialists among the fastest-growing roles and data entry clerks among the declining ones. So the pressure falls on routine work, and the growth sits in people who can reason about data.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li><strong>Now:</strong> use an AI tool for first drafts of code and summaries, and write down every check you ran on its output.</li>
    <li><strong>Next:</strong> turn a repeated task from your own project into a small checked workflow, with a note on what could go wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Later:</strong> as tools improve, the paid work moves to framing the question, validation and explanation. Build that layer early.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    There is no promise of a fixed multiplier here. AI helps most when you already know what a right answer looks like. In other words, the maths you learn is the quality control.
  </p>

  <h2 id="business-layer">The business layer: who pays a maths graduate directly</h2>

  <p>
    Every route has a layer most people are never taught: who pays, and how that person finds and trusts you. Here are the basics, with no income promised.
  </p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    {salesSteps.map((s) => (
