    ))}
  </ol>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership paths to notice:</strong> analytics or optimisation work for small firms, and tutoring that grows into cohorts or recorded courses. They scale because you own the price, you can systemise the work or add people later, and repeat clients or batches keep paying without a new hunt each month. A salary is a tool to save and learn. Ownership of an offer, a client list or a course is what builds wealth over time.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    For fixed government posts such as the Indian Statistical Service or RBI, the sales layer is employer-only. It matters for the side route, such as tutoring beside the preparation, or for the move after the post.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skills">Build a high-value skill portfolio: core, multiplier, proof</h2>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    <li><strong>One core skill.</strong> Pick the branch you can go deepest in: statistics and probability, optimisation, or number theory and algebra. Depth is what filters out casual competition.</li>
    <li><strong>One multiplier.</strong> Python with SQL for most routes, C or C++ for cryptography. Add one domain, such as insurance or logistics, so you solve a named problem.</li>
    <li><strong>Visible proof.</strong> A public notebook, a case note, a solved set of past papers or a recorded demo class. Use one platform well rather than five half-built profiles. Then meet people who do the work and ask one specific question with your proof attached.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain English.</strong> Practise writing a result in five sentences for someone who does not study maths. Clear English is a multiplier in every route here.</li>
    <li><strong>A judged course, not a pile.</strong> Check the instructor, the last update, the syllabus, the exercises and the feedback. A certificate is weak proof unless the employer asks for it.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Work style also matters. Data and tutoring work can be done remotely. Research and many government jobs cannot.
  </p>

